<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  THE SAME APPLICATION, BUILT THE OTHER WAY
  ============================================================================
  Read this file next to ../pom.xml. They produce comparable programs against
  the SAME PostgreSQL database, and almost nothing in the two build files
  looks alike. That difference is the lesson; fieldbook chapters 16-18 are the
  prose version of it.

  Three things to notice before anything else.

  1. THIS IS NOT A MODULE OF THE ROOT POM.
     The root project is `packaging>war` and builds on its own. Making it an
     aggregator (`packaging>pom` + `<modules>`) would have changed what
     `mvn package` does at the top level, and the whole point of this exercise
     is that the Jakarta EE build keeps working untouched. So this is a
     separate, independent Maven project that happens to live in the same
     repository:

         mvn package              (in the repo root)  -> enrollment.war
         mvn package -f spring-service/pom.xml        -> enrollment-spring.jar

     One repository, two deployables. That is also, not by coincidence, the
     shape a team is in on the first day of a "we are moving to microservices"
     project.

  2. THE PARENT REPLACES ~200 LINES OF VERSION MANAGEMENT.
     ../pom.xml declares a version property for every single dependency, and a
     long comment explaining which of them are allowed to move independently
     (they mirror what WildFly ships) and which are free (they are build-local).
     Here, `spring-boot-starter-parent` has already made every one of those
     decisions and tested the combination. Look below: almost no <version> tags.
     That is not magic, it is a curated BOM - and it is the single biggest
     day-to-day difference between the two ecosystems.

  3. `provided` IS GONE.
     Over there, the Jakarta EE API is `provided` because WildFly owns the
     implementation at runtime - see the long comment in ../pom.xml about why
     shipping your own copy causes LinkageErrors. Here there is no server to
     provide anything: `spring-boot-starter-web` PACKAGES an embedded Tomcat
     into the jar. The application server stopped being infrastructure you
     install and became a library you depend on. Everything else about Spring
     Boot follows from that one decision.
  ============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      THE PARENT.

      A Maven parent is ordinary inheritance: this POM inherits that POM's
      properties, dependencyManagement, pluginManagement and build settings.
      `spring-boot-starter-parent` is doing four jobs at once, and it is worth
      being able to name them in an interview:

        1. dependencyManagement for ~2000 artifacts (the spring-boot-dependencies
           BOM). This is why <dependency> blocks below carry no <version>.
        2. Sensible plugin configuration - the compiler release, the resource
           filtering, UTF-8 encoding, and the repackage goal.
        3. A `java.version` property that sets the compiler release.
        4. Defaults such as `maven.compiler.parameters=true`, which the root
           POM has to ask for by hand with <arg>-parameters</arg>.

      THE TRAP: inheriting a parent is not the only way to get the BOM. If your
      company already has a corporate parent POM (very common in Italian
      enterprise shops), you import the BOM in <dependencyManagement> with
      <scope>import</scope> instead. Knowing both forms is a real interview
      question, and the answer "you can only have one parent, but you can import
      as many BOMs as you like" is the whole of it.
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.0</version>
        <relativePath/> <!-- empty: look the parent up in the repository, not on disk -->
    </parent>

    <groupId>it.unicam.cs.enrollment</groupId>
    <artifactId>enrollment-spring</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <!--
      `jar`, not `war`. The output is an executable "fat jar": your classes,
      your dependencies, and a launcher, all in one file that `java -jar` runs.
      There is no deployment step and nothing to install first. Compare with
      ../pom.xml, where the WAR is deliberately almost empty because everything
      it needs is already inside WildFly.
    -->
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>UNICAM Course Enrollment Service (Spring Boot)</name>
    <description>
        The enrollment domain from the Jakarta EE application, re-implemented on
        Spring Boot against the same PostgreSQL schema. Built to be diffed
        against ../src/main/java, not to replace it.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!--
          Inherited from the parent, overridden here to match the root project.
          Both applications compile for Java 21, so a comparison between them is
          about the framework and nothing else.
        -->
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>

        <!--
          THE THREE VERSIONS THE BOOT BOM DOES NOT MANAGE.

          Everything else in this file is versionless because
          spring-boot-dependencies has already picked a tested combination.
          These three are outside it, so they are pinned here - and the fact
          that there are only three is the point of the parent.

          springdoc in particular is version-sensitive: 2.x is for Boot 3.x and
          1.x is for Boot 2.x, and mixing them produces a startup failure about
          a missing class rather than anything mentioning versions.
        -->
        <version.springdoc>2.8.5</version.springdoc>
        <version.mapstruct>1.6.3</version.mapstruct>
        <version.resilience4j>2.3.0</version.resilience4j>
        <version.flapdoodle>4.20.0</version.flapdoodle>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>

        <!-- ================================================================
             THE WEB LAYER
             ================================================================
             Brings in Spring MVC, Jackson (JSON), and an embedded Tomcat.

             The Jakarta EE equivalent of this line is: install WildFly.

             Note which JSON library arrives. Over there it is JSON-B (Yasson),
             the Jakarta standard; here it is Jackson, a de-facto standard that
             predates it. They disagree about several defaults - see the
             comments on ProblemDetail and application.yml - and "why is my
             field missing from the JSON" is the same question with two
             different answers depending on which side of the wire you are on.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             PERSISTENCE
             ================================================================
             Spring Data JPA, which brings Hibernate, the Jakarta Persistence
             API, spring-tx, and HikariCP (the connection pool).

             The pool matters more than it looks. In the Jakarta EE application
             the datasource and its pool are configured INSIDE the server
             (docker/wildfly/configure.cli) and the application only ever sees a
             JNDI name. Here the pool is a library, configured in
             application.yml, and it is your problem. Fieldbook chapter 25 makes
             the argument that the pool - not the CPU - is your real concurrency
             limit; this is where you would act on it.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             BEAN VALIDATION
             ================================================================
             NOT included by starter-web since Boot 2.3. Forgetting this
             dependency is the classic silent failure: @Valid on a controller
             method compiles perfectly and then does absolutely nothing at
             runtime, because there is no validator on the classpath to run it.
             Every invalid request sails through to the service layer.

             Over in the Jakarta EE application this cannot happen: Bean
             Validation is part of the platform, so it is in
             jakarta.jakartaee-api whether you think about it or not.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             ACTUATOR - health, info and metrics without writing any
             ================================================================
             Fieldbook chapter 33 explains liveness and readiness probes and
             then has no endpoint to point at, because the Jakarta EE
             application does not have one. This dependency is that endpoint:

                 /actuator/health/liveness    "am I wedged?"   - must NOT touch the DB
                 /actuator/health/readiness   "can I serve?"   - SHOULD touch the DB

             Boot separates those two for you, correctly, out of the box. Getting
             them the wrong way round is one of the most common production
             incidents in the industry, and this starter is the reason most
             Spring teams never have to think about it.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             OPENAPI / SWAGGER UI
             ================================================================
             Zero mentions in the fieldbook, on a large share of Java adverts,
             and roughly fifteen minutes of work. springdoc reads the
             annotations that are ALREADY on the controllers - @GetMapping,
             @PathVariable, @Valid, the Bean Validation constraints, the record
             components - and produces an OpenAPI 3 document from them.

                 http://localhost:8281/v3/api-docs     the JSON specification
                 http://localhost:8281/swagger-ui.html an interactive console

             THE ARGUMENT WORTH HAVING: a specification generated from the code
             cannot drift from the code, which is the failure mode of every
             hand-written API document. The counter-argument - design-first,
             where the YAML is written before the code and the server is
             generated from it - is what large organisations with many teams
             actually do, because it lets the contract be agreed before either
             side is built. Knowing that both exist, and why a five-team company
             picks the second, is the interview answer.

             The Jakarta EE equivalent is MicroProfile OpenAPI, which WildFly
             ships and this project does not switch on. That is a gap on the
             other side, recorded in the backlog.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
            <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
            <version>${version.springdoc}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             CACHING
             ================================================================
             starter-cache is the ABSTRACTION - @Cacheable, @CacheEvict and a
             CacheManager interface. Caffeine is one implementation of it.

             That separation is the whole design, and it is why the annotations
             below never mention Caffeine: swap this dependency for
             spring-boot-starter-data-redis and the same annotated methods start
             using a shared network cache instead of a local in-memory one, with
             no code change. Local first is also the right default - a cache that
             does not survive a restart is much harder to get wrong than one
             that does.

             Caffeine rather than Redis here so the cache is testable with no
             Docker daemon. The Redis configuration is written out in
             application.yml, commented, next to the reason you would move.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.ben-manes.caffeine</groupId>
            <artifactId>caffeine</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             METRICS - Micrometer to Prometheus
             ================================================================
             Micrometer is to metrics what SLF4J is to logging: a facade. Your
             code calls Micrometer; this artifact decides that the numbers come
             out in Prometheus format at /actuator/prometheus. Swap it for the
             Datadog or CloudWatch registry and nothing you wrote changes.

             Actuator already publishes JVM memory, GC pauses, thread counts,
             HTTP request timings per endpoint and status, and HikariCP pool
             usage - that last one being the metric fieldbook chapter 25 argues
             is your real concurrency limit. Adding one line of dependency
             turns "the application feels slow" into a graph.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             MAPSTRUCT - the mapper you do not write
             ================================================================
             An ANNOTATION PROCESSOR, not a runtime library. At compile time it
             reads an annotated interface and generates a plain Java class that
             implements it, field by field. You can read the output in
             target/generated-sources/annotations - and reading it once is the
             fastest way to stop thinking of it as magic.

             No reflection, no proxies, no startup cost: the generated code is
             the same assignments you would have typed.

             CourseMapper is left hand-written and EnrollmentMapper is generated,
             deliberately, so the two can be compared side by side. See the
             comment on EnrollmentMapper for which one each situation deserves.

             The processor has to be declared in annotationProcessorPaths below
             as well as here, and forgetting that produces the single most
             confusing MapStruct error: the interface compiles, the
             implementation class is never generated, and Spring fails at
             startup saying no bean of that type exists.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mapstruct</groupId>
            <artifactId>mapstruct</artifactId>
            <version>${version.mapstruct}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             MONGODB - the document store, as a read model
             ================================================================
             NoSQL is on the advert and appears in the fieldbook as one
             paragraph. This is it made concrete, and the shape matters more
             than the dependency: MongoDB is used here as a READ MODEL beside
             PostgreSQL, not instead of it.

             That is the honest architecture and it is what the chapter 33
             answer already implies. The enrollment path needs a unique
             constraint on (student, course) and a transaction that counts seats
             under a lock; a document store makes both materially harder. The
             reporting path needs flexible, denormalised documents that a
             dashboard reads whole. Different jobs, different tools, and using
             both is far more common in industry than picking one.

             POLYGLOT PERSISTENCE is the name for it, and the caveat worth
             stating alongside: two databases is two things to operate, back up,
             monitor and keep consistent. It is a real cost, and "the relational
             database is the default and the burden of proof is on the
             alternative" is still the right instinct.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-mongodb</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             RESILIENCE4J - circuit breaker, retry, time limiter
             ================================================================
             Needed the moment a method call became a network call. Fieldbook
             chapter 33 names the circuit breaker and never shows one; this is
             the dependency that lets NotificationClient have one.

             THE PATTERN IN ONE SENTENCE: a circuit breaker stops calling a
             service that is already failing, so your threads are not all
             blocked waiting on a timeout for a dependency you know is down.
             Without it, one slow downstream service exhausts your thread pool
             and takes YOUR service down with it - which is how a single failure
             becomes an outage across five services.

             Spring Cloud Circuit Breaker is the other artifact you will see
             named. It is a thin abstraction OVER Resilience4j, useful if you
             might swap implementations, and one more layer to explain if you
             will not. Using Resilience4j directly is the common choice.

             Hystrix is the name in older material. It has been end-of-life
             since 2018 and Resilience4j is its replacement - saying that when
             an interviewer mentions Hystrix is a small, real signal.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.github.resilience4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>resilience4j-spring-boot3</artifactId>
            <version>${version.resilience4j}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!-- The AOP support the annotations need. Without it @CircuitBreaker
             and @Retry compile and do absolutely nothing - the same silent
             failure as forgetting starter-validation. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-aop</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             THE POSTGRESQL DRIVER  -  scope: runtime
             ================================================================
             `runtime` means: not on the compile classpath, but present when the
             program runs. Correct, because no line of your code names a
             PostgreSQL class - you talk to java.sql / JPA interfaces and the
             driver registers itself. If you can `import org.postgresql.*` you
             have almost certainly made a mistake.

             Compare ../pom.xml, where this same driver appears only as a
             dependency of the Flyway PLUGIN, because WildFly supplies it at
             runtime as a server module. Same jar, three different scopes,
             depending on who owns the runtime.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================
             TESTS
             ================================================================
             ONE dependency replaces the six the root POM declares by hand
             (JUnit, Mockito, AssertJ, the JAX-RS implementation, Hibernate
             Validator, an SLF4J binding). starter-test brings JUnit 5, Mockito,
             AssertJ, Hamcrest, JSONPath, and Spring's own test support.

             Note what is NOT needed here: the root POM has a long comment about
             adding resteasy-core to tests because `Response.ok(...)` throws
             without a JAX-RS implementation on the classpath, and another about
             adding Yasson so Hibernate can find a JSON-B provider. Both of those
             are the `provided` scope biting back - an annotation-only classpath
             cannot execute anything. Boot never has that problem because nothing
             here is `provided` in the first place.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          H2, for the repository tests.

          Same choice, and the same caveat, as the root project: an in-memory
          database makes @DataJpaTest run in milliseconds with no Docker, and it
          IS NOT PostgreSQL. Fieldbook chapter 20 calls this "the test that
          lies", and the honest answer is to have both - H2 for the fast loop,
          Testcontainers for the truth. Both are below.
        -->
        <!--
          H2 at RUNTIME, not test, for one specific reason.

          Scope `test` would be correct for a production service. It is `runtime`
          here so that the `demo` profile can start the whole application with no
          PostgreSQL, no Docker and no setup at all:

              mvn spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.profiles=demo

          That matters for a teaching repository: somebody reading chapter 16
          should be able to see the API answer without first debugging a
          container runtime. The cost is about 2.5 MB of driver in the jar that
          production never loads, and the honest note that a real service would
          not ship it.

          The default profile still points at PostgreSQL. Nothing about the
          normal path changed.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
            <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Testcontainers: a REAL PostgreSQL 16, started by the test, thrown away
          afterwards. This is what makes an integration test honest - the same
          engine, the same dialect, the same behaviour for the pessimistic lock
          that H2 fakes.

          No <version> on any of these: spring-boot-dependencies manages the
          Testcontainers BOM too.

          `spring-boot-testcontainers` is the Boot 3.1+ glue that provides
          @ServiceConnection, which wires the container's random port and
          credentials into the DataSource with no property plumbing at all.
          Before it existed, every project hand-wrote a
          @DynamicPropertySource block; you will still meet that in older code.

          These tests SKIP when there is no Docker daemon, rather than failing -
          see AbstractPostgresIT. A test that cannot run is not a test that
          failed, and pretending otherwise trains people to ignore red builds.
        -->
        <!--
          AN EMBEDDED MongoDB for tests - a real mongod binary, downloaded once
          and run as a child process. No Docker.

          Chosen deliberately over a Testcontainers Mongo so the document tests
          run in the SAME fast loop as everything else, on a machine with no
          container runtime. The trade is the usual one: it is a real MongoDB,
          so the queries are genuinely executed, but it is a single node - no
          replica set, and therefore no multi-document transactions, which
          MongoDB only supports on a replica set.

          That limitation is not an inconvenience here. It is the lesson: if
          your design needs a transaction across documents, the document store
          is fighting you, and the relational database was the right answer.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.flapdoodle.embed</groupId>
            <artifactId>de.flapdoodle.embed.mongo.spring3x</artifactId>
            <version>${version.flapdoodle}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          FLYWAY, test-scoped only.

          This is what makes EnrollmentApiIT genuinely honest rather than merely
          slow. Without it the container would get a schema Hibernate invented
          from the entity classes in THIS module - so the test would prove that
          the mappings agree with themselves, which is not a claim worth making.

          With it, the migrations in ../src/main/resources/db/migration run
          against the container first, and then `ddl-auto: validate` checks these
          entities against the schema the OTHER application actually deploys. If
          someone adds a column here and forgets the migration, this test fails.
          That is the only mechanism in the repository that can catch the two
          projects drifting apart.

          `test` scope, so nothing Flyway-related is packaged. The root project
          makes the same choice for the same reason and explains it at length:
          migrating at application startup races when there is more than one
          instance. See the flyway profile in ../pom.xml.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <finalName>enrollment-spring</finalName>

        <plugins>
            <!--
              THE REPACKAGE PLUGIN.

              `mvn package` normally produces a plain jar containing only your
              classes - which cannot run, because nothing tells the JVM where
              Spring is. This plugin runs afterwards and rewrites it into an
              executable "fat jar": your classes under BOOT-INF/classes, every
              dependency under BOOT-INF/lib, and a small launcher in the root
              that builds a classloader over them.

              It also renames the original to enrollment-spring.jar.original,
              which is a useful thing to have seen once - it is the ordinary jar
              Maven built before Boot got hold of it.

              Version and configuration both come from the parent. Declaring the
              plugin at all is the only part that is ours.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              THE ANNOTATION PROCESSOR PATH, for MapStruct.

              An annotation processor has to be on a separate classpath from the
              dependencies, so declaring mapstruct-processor as an ordinary
              <dependency> does NOT work reliably - it may run, it may not,
              depending on the compiler plugin version. This is the supported way.

              The two compiler arguments are worth setting from the start:

                unmappedTargetPolicy=ERROR turns "you forgot to map a field" from
                a warning nobody reads into a build failure. Without it, adding a
                component to a DTO silently leaves it null in every response - a
                real bug that ships regularly.

                componentModel=spring makes the generated class a @Component, so
                Spring can inject it like any other bean. Without it you would
                have to call Mappers.getMapper() by hand.

              Note the -parameters argument is NOT here: the Spring Boot parent
              already sets it, which is why the record DTOs deserialise without
              @JsonProperty. The root POM has to ask for it explicitly.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <annotationProcessorPaths>
                        <path>
                            <groupId>org.mapstruct</groupId>
                            <artifactId>mapstruct-processor</artifactId>
                            <version>${version.mapstruct}</version>
                        </path>
                    </annotationProcessorPaths>
                    <compilerArgs>
                        <arg>-Amapstruct.unmappedTargetPolicy=ERROR</arg>
                        <arg>-Amapstruct.defaultComponentModel=spring</arg>
                    </compilerArgs>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              Failsafe, for *IT classes - exactly the same split as the root
              project. Surefire runs unit tests on `mvn test`; Failsafe runs
              integration tests on `mvn verify`, after packaging. Developers run
              the first all day, CI runs the second.

              Unlike the root POM this does not need a <version>: the parent
              manages it.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>integration-test</goal>
                            <goal>verify</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
